server: Merge queued WM_MOUSEHWHEEL messages as WM_MOUSEWHEEL ones are.

GitHub Neutron#31 (ThinkPad touchpad, Premiere Pro 2026), on 11.10-148: "it's still way too easy to overwhelm the
scrolling if you move too fast, and this gets even worse if you're running on power saving mode. It feels like if the
inputs are processed so slowly that they queue up to the point that you're left waiting for it to settle."

merge_message merged a queued wheel message into the previous one (same window, same pointer position) only for
WM_MOUSEWHEEL. Windows does it for WM_MOUSEHWHEEL too. Premiere scrolls its timeline one step per wheel message, so when
it fell behind a fast sideways swipe every queued message became a step of its own, played back after the swipe.

Measured on Windows 11 build 26200 (his machine):
- harnesses/wheel-merge.c (10 wheel events through SendInput while the app does not read its queue): vertical notches
  1 message (-1200), horizontal notches 1 message (+1200), small steps of 15 one message each axis (-150 / +150).
- harnesses/touchpad-wheel.c with a real trackpad: an app spending 100 ms per message gets merged messages on both axes
  (up to +14880 horizontal, +7680 vertical).
Wine before this patch: vertical merged, horizontal 10 messages. With it: the Windows table (gate off: as before).

Lab Premiere 2026 (clean-room AE2026, 11.10-148, fake smooth scrolling, Premiere's process paused while 30 sideways
events arrive, then resumed): 148 played back 30 steps (1800 px); with this patch one move, the view lands about a
screen further (0-20 s -> 20-40 s), as a vertical swipe already did. Ordinary sideways scrolling unchanged (8 steps
of 34 px each way for 120 units).
Kill switch NEUTRON_HWHEEL_MERGE=0 (read by wineserver).

--- a/server/queue.c
+++ b/server/queue.c
@@ -856,7 +856,15 @@
     return prev;
 }
 
-/* try to merge a WM_MOUSEWHEEL message with the last in the list; return 1 if successful */
+/* NEUTRON hwheel-merge: see merge_message */
+static int neutron_hwheel_merge(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    if (on < 0) { const char *e = getenv( "NEUTRON_HWHEEL_MERGE" ); on = !(e && e[0] == '0'); }
+    return on;
+}
+
+/* try to merge a WM_MOUSEWHEEL or WM_MOUSEHWHEEL message with the last in the list; return 1 if successful */
 static int merge_mousewheel( struct thread_input *input, const struct message *msg )
 {
     struct message *prev;
@@ -937,6 +945,13 @@
 static int merge_message( struct thread_input *input, const struct message *msg )
 {
     if (msg->msg == WM_MOUSEWHEEL) return merge_mousewheel( input, msg );
+    /* NEUTRON hwheel-merge (GitHub Neutron#31): Windows merges queued WM_MOUSEHWHEEL messages the same way. Measured on
+     * Windows 11 build 26200 (harnesses/wheel-merge.c): 10 horizontal notches sent while the app is busy arrive as one
+     * message of +1200 (10 small steps of 15: one of +150), as the vertical ones do; a real trackpad's stream reaches a
+     * slow app merged on both axes (touchpad-wheel.c: up to +14880 in one message). Here they arrived one by one, and
+     * Premiere Pro, which scrolls one step per wheel message, kept scrolling long after a fast sideways swipe ("they queue
+     * up to the point that you're left waiting for it to settle"). Kill switch NEUTRON_HWHEEL_MERGE=0. */
+    if (msg->msg == WM_MOUSEHWHEEL && neutron_hwheel_merge()) return merge_mousewheel( input, msg );
     if (msg->msg == WM_MOUSEMOVE) return merge_mousemove( input, msg );
     if (msg->msg == WM_WINE_CLIPCURSOR) return merge_unique_message( input, WM_WINE_CLIPCURSOR, msg );
     if (msg->msg == WM_WINE_SETCURSOR) return merge_unique_message( input, WM_WINE_SETCURSOR, msg );
